{"title":"2024 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 20","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_20\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 20</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Pi_Academy\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Pi Academy</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3_by_grogg007_(5_mins_⏩)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3 by grogg007 (5 mins ⏩)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Scholars_Foundation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Scholars Foundation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_solution_by_TheNeuralMathAcademy\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video solution by TheNeuralMathAcademy</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_20\">Problem 20</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 20\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be a subset of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/1/cd1bcb429e98e69ab2cfcaa919f33240b37c077f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, 3, \\dots, 2024\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"140\" height=\"20\" /> such that the following two conditions hold:\n</p>\n<ul><li>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> are distinct elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/b/15bbc7e597b22fbfed716d4fd30f5d01c5716ec5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|x-y| &gt; 2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"92\" height=\"20\" /></li></ul>\n<ul><li>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> are distinct odd elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/2/2020fae8f73e0673aafdb9df46a191e4750c1ce2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|x-y| &gt; 6.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"92\" height=\"20\" /></li></ul>\n<p>What is the maximum possible number of elements in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/5/1b5b9480d9fb626762ffca695027b77a5d84a3a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }436 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) }506 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) }608 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) }654 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) }675$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"446\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>All lists are organized in ascending order:\n</p><p>By listing out the smallest possible elements of subset <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/2/2f2e8e8dd78b26ddc9ac278cc179101d30644cae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"16\" /> we can find that subset <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> starts with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b26810db4f0f1f62065cc099bf767e3cdcf1fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 4, 8, 11, 14, 18, 21, 24, 28, 31, \\dots\\}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"285\" height=\"20\" /> It is easily noticed that the elements of the subset \"loop around\" every 3 elements, specifically adding 10 each time. This means that there will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/b/f6be450672fc379be12787485ffd5dce50fdff22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2024/10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"65\" height=\"20\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/9/3c9e3c2989e737d563f5d873e4321e4dbe1c79fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$202R4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" /> whole loops in the subset <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/2/2f2e8e8dd78b26ddc9ac278cc179101d30644cae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"16\" /> implying that there will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/d/2bdb5897b2782876e71f3db163b6445a0c4434bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$202*3 = 606$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"107\" height=\"14\" /> elements in S. However, we have undercounted, as we did not count the remainder that resulted from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/b/f6be450672fc379be12787485ffd5dce50fdff22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2024/10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"65\" height=\"20\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/2/d52dd46b7fc4df2f724b5af4311a675147cd6e8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$.$\" width=\"3\" height=\"2\" /> With a remainder of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/5/4054552638cf9f29e857a1306bd3ec31df3c19b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"15\" /> we can fit <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> more elements into the subset <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/2/2f2e8e8dd78b26ddc9ac278cc179101d30644cae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"16\" /> namely <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/9/e99b9c3842e40e4102ae86b3317f26103c80cdb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2021$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/7/df78b0f2f41d4a4758808f92984239e83d2a7822.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2024,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"17\" /> resulting in a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/8/388d9c1fbf446236cc6226323c70e54f66ef0a29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$606+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"60\" height=\"15\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/9/579da4e984c200d040d0f6a84210689710d9230e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }608}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"75\" height=\"30\" /> elements in subset <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n<b>NOTE:</b>\n</p><p>To prove that this is the best we can do, consider adding each element one by one, for the first element, say n. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/b/f3b9b7a46559a729577c3ca04dd2d7e66de16b3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, we can choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/8/438ca8cfe4ef1f8af9be1b9ba6670a87cced8f11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n - 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> which is always better. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/6/c766f9fce6070981e62f805a7cf7294baed4498e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/e/c6ebb21f2ef991fc6e2ab54d7335cc6d1ea15ab0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 2.$\" width=\"47\" height=\"12\" /> \n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/8/598a16d4b7dde3dec3a390109882d54bb0308ea5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 2$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> was optimal, then choose it, then the set of usable numbers in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> becomes 5 through 2024. We can transform the usable set of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> contains the numbers 1 through 2020. Because we assumed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/8/598a16d4b7dde3dec3a390109882d54bb0308ea5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 2$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> was optimal, we can choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/8/598a16d4b7dde3dec3a390109882d54bb0308ea5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 2$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> for the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> too. Because every element in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> is 4 below the elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, choosing 2 in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> would mean choosing 6 in set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. By induction we see that our list would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/2/b52f64e8189961fde384c05aaf5866be615b3f97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{2,6,10,14,18,.....2022\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"195\" height=\"20\" /> which only gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/d/fdd6c0548bc557b9867499c8e0ae17751d9c6c12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$506$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> elements which is sub-optimal. Therefore, we can conclude that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/6/c766f9fce6070981e62f805a7cf7294baed4498e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> is optimal, and we proceed as the solution above.\n</p><p>-weihou0\n</p><p>- minor latex edits by eqb5000\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that we can first place odd numbers, then place even numbers between each pair. We can start at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and continue from there. Realize that the smallest number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/8/1580dc19398ee78770fd5b2f1aa86acddaddf55c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$kx+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" /> reproduces odd number is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. The next ones are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/2/b8276430fa9973aaf3f313b52047f23238f10b11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10, 12, 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"17\" />. We can proceed to find the number of numbers in this particular sequence. From the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/4/464c6b5d3ed8eff50acaf7a86127cdd4872763ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8x+1=2023$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"113\" height=\"15\" />, we get that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/d/50d25a6a737d17b2606ede557b2446bc3d706033.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\leq 252.875$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"96\" height=\"16\" /> works, so this means there is 253 solutions. Looking at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/7/fc7b89a1cd4ed59e6feac6d08b63dacfb84fb536.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2,3,4,5,6,7,9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"131\" height=\"17\" /> we can see that there could only be 1 possible number between each pair, yielding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/3/a4357972be11724ab8b511d433af36d6e6f1e5b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$252+253=505$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"130\" height=\"15\" />. Then see that we can fit two more into the number count since the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/1/5617f6d8def6056c5921630439d64b56cfa519ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2017$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"36\" height=\"13\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/7/e5711c8d31d3bc3119aba377c9bda0f9dfb14e4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2024$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"36\" height=\"13\" /> can fit two evens. Now this means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> don’t work. Now test out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/6/f06f49e90998c5521bb568e525ae83cf16801785.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10x+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"62\" height=\"15\" />. Using the same method, we get that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/a/2aac2990435d7892ddb0bb8f80e3770ba983400d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$608$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> is the maximum number in the set. Everything above <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/1/ec186fd1809357d1f530bee2334ceb59d405b4f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=10$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" /> doesn’t work, as we can split it down into smaller subgroups, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/9/579da4e984c200d040d0f6a84210689710d9230e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }608}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"75\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~EaZ_Shadow\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We find the following pairs of numbers work: \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/e/f7e22f5ab585a8e9ac982cb4dfcd4bb1681989b1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(1,4), (4,8), (8,11), (11,14), (14, 18), (18, 21), (21, 24) \\dots\\]\" width=\"434\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><br />\nCall a number an OE (odd-even) pair if the first number in the pair is odd and the second is even. Do the same for EE (even-even) and EO (even-odd) pairs. Notice that we're adding 10 to the pairs in each set, so there are 404 numbers in each set of pairs <b>excluding 1 and 2024:</b>\n</p><p><br />\n<b>OE pairs:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/f/0df3ce21b8d2577b633be7174aeb4b31257ce11f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(1,4), (11,14), ... , (2021,2024) \\implies (202 + 1) \\cdot 2 - 2 = 404\\]\" width=\"459\" height=\"18\" /> \n</p><p><br />\n<b>EE pairs:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/2/fc28939e006c60970e20d7765275941792ec0823.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(4,8), (14,18), ..., (2014,2018)  \\implies (201 + 1) \\cdot 2 = 404\\]\" width=\"427\" height=\"18\" /> \n</p><p><br />\n<b>EO pairs:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/3/363e9756a6c9294fcda240fec93f694587960229.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(8,11), (18,21), ..., (2018, 2021) \\implies (201 + 1) \\cdot 2 = 404\\]\" width=\"436\" height=\"18\" />  \n</p><p><br />\nEvery number besides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/7/e5711c8d31d3bc3119aba377c9bda0f9dfb14e4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2024$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"36\" height=\"13\" /> is being overcounted twice (for example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is counted twice in the EE and OE pairs), so we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/9/1a9c96aa7cce8d26a940924e70319c2171d135e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$404 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} = 606.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"112\" height=\"38\" /> Finally, we add <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (adding back <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/7/e5711c8d31d3bc3119aba377c9bda0f9dfb14e4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2024$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"36\" height=\"13\" />) to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/9/579da4e984c200d040d0f6a84210689710d9230e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }608}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"75\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:grogg007\">grogg007</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Power Solve\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NZ0SBMqeAfg\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NZ0SBMqeAfg</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Pi_Academy\">Video Solution by Pi Academy</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Pi Academy\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/fW7OGWee31c?si=oq7toGPh2QaksLHE\">https://youtu.be/fW7OGWee31c?si=oq7toGPh2QaksLHE</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/BhiczrT7Sg0?si=XnkHOJl5n9SWHsfc\">https://youtu.be/BhiczrT7Sg0?si=XnkHOJl5n9SWHsfc</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_3_by_grogg007_.285_mins_.E2.8F.A9.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3_by_grogg007_(5_mins_⏩)\">Video Solution 3 by <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:grogg007\">grogg007</a> (5 mins ⏩)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 3 by grogg007 (5 mins ⏩)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/rFU5EW9VOq8\">https://youtu.be/rFU5EW9VOq8</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Scholars_Foundation\">Video Solution by Scholars Foundation</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Scholars Foundation\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FKOqZau--5w&amp;t=1s\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FKOqZau--5w&amp;t=1s</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_solution_by_TheNeuralMathAcademy\">Video solution by TheNeuralMathAcademy</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video solution by TheNeuralMathAcademy\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4b_YLnyegtw&amp;t=4268s\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4b_YLnyegtw&amp;t=4268s</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?